The U.S. state of [[Maryland]] first required its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s in 1904. Registrants provided their own [[Vehicle registration plate|license plate]]s for display until 1910, when the state began to issue plates.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-maryland-license-plates-vintage-maryland-license-plates/ |title=Old Maryland License Plates |publisher=LeatherLicensePlates.com |access-date=May 30, 2017 |archive-date=March 4, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170304112455/http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-maryland-license-plates-vintage-maryland-license-plates/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Plates are currently issued by the Motor Vehicle Administration of the [[Maryland Department of Transportation]]. Front and rear plates are required for most classes of vehicles, while only rear plates are required for motorcycles and trailers, as well as classes L (historic, 20+ years and unaltered) and N (street rod, 25+ years and altered) that are at least 50 years old.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://mva.maryland.gov/vehicles/Pages/licenseplates/general.aspx |title=General License Plate Information |website=Maryland Department of Transportation Motor Vehicle Administration |access-date=December 23, 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.dmv.com/md/maryland/custom-vehicle-registration |title=Custom Car Registration in Maryland |date=March 9, 2020 |website=DMV.com |access-date=December 24, 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://mva.maryland.gov/Documents/VR-096.pdf |title=Application for Historic or Street Rod Registration |website=Maryland Department of Transportation Motor Vehicle Administration |access-date=December 24, 2022 }}</ref>

==Passenger baseplates==
===1910 to 1970===
In 1956, the United States, Canada, and Mexico came to an agreement with the [[American Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators]], the [[Automobile Manufacturers Association]] and the [[National Safety Council]] that standardized the size for license plates for vehicles (except those for [[motorcycle]]s) at {{convert|6|in|cm}} in height by {{convert|12|in|cm}} in width, with standardized mounting holes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Garrish |first=Christopher |date=October 2016 |title=Reconsidering the Standard Plate Size |magazine=Plates |publisher=[[Automobile License Plate Collectors Association]] |volume=62 |number=5}}</ref> The 1956 (expired March 31, 1957) issue was the first Maryland license plate that complied with these standards.
